Cellulitis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Unilateral spreading erythema, Localized warmth/tenderness, Fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-012
Reason for Visit: Rapidly expanding, hot, painful, red rash on the lower leg accompanied by fever and chills.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an acute onset of rapidly spreading, painful lower limb erythema with associated systemic symptoms (fever, rigors) over the past 12 hours. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate simple cellulitis from necrotizing fasciitis, initiate targeted, high-dose systemic antibiotic therapy, and prevent the progression to overt sepsis, which could not safely wait for the next routine daytime appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My leg is swollen, burning hot, and hurts to walk on, and I've been shivering with a fever all afternoon."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports noticing a small insect bite on the right lower shin 48 hours ago. Over the last 12 hours, there has been a rapidly expanding area of diffuse, poorly demarcated skin erythema extending from the ankle to the mid-calf. The area is exquisitely tender on palpation. The patient describes sudden onset high fever, chills, and systemic malaise starting 4 hours ago. They report a unilateral distribution affecting only the right lower extremity. The rapid upward spread of the redness and the onset of uncontrollable shivering prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently struggling to bear weight on the right leg due to pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Rapid systemic deterioration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 112 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Flushed, diaphoretic, shivering, appearing systemically unwell but fully conscious and alert.
Physical Exam: The right lower leg demonstrates a 15x10 cm area of confluent, blanching erythema extending from the medial malleolus to the mid-anterior shin. The skin exhibits localized oedema, intense warmth, and exquisite tenderness on palpation. A prominent peau d'orange appearance is visible due to superficial dermal oedema. A small, crusted insect bite acts as the visible portal of entry on the lower shin. There is tender regional lymphadenopathy in the right inguinal region. No crepitus, purple bullae, severe pain out of proportion to exam, or signs of necrotizing fasciitis.
Environmental Observations: Clean home. Patient has been attempting to rest with the leg down.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current antibiotics or immunosuppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Unilateral Lower Limb Cellulitis with systemic inflammatory response - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Necrotizing Fasciitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of violaceous bullae, cutaneous anesthesia, palpable crepitus, or extreme pain out of proportion to the superficial physical findings.

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of high fever, demarcated superficial erythema, extreme local heat, and a clear cutaneous portal of entry, which strongly favor an infectious etiology over a deep venous occlusion.

Stasis Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, rapid unilateral onset with high fever and systemic toxicity. Stasis dermatitis is chronic, bilateral, and non-infectious.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to systemic involvement)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classical signs of acute lower limb cellulitis: rapidly spreading unilateral erythema, localized heat, and significant systemic toxicity (fever, tachycardia, rigors). Triage correctly recognized the systemic signs and rapid spread as requiring urgent out-of-hours evaluation to rule out deep space or necrotizing infections. Clinical examination confirms a severe superficial bacterial infection stemming from a traumatic portal of entry. Immediate administration of high-dose systemic antibiotics and strict instructions on limb elevation were mandatory to halt the proximal spread of the infection and prevent the progression to overt sepsis, averting an immediate hospital admission if treated aggressively now.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Cephalexin 1g stat. Prescribed Cephalexin 500mg QID for 7-10 days. Marked the current border of the erythema with a surgical pen to monitor progression.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking your antibiotics exactly four times a day, evenly spaced, you will keep a constant level of medicine in your bloodstream, rapidly killing the spreading bacteria. By keeping your leg elevated above the level of your heart at all times when resting, you will use gravity to drain the painful swelling, which will dramatically reduce the throbbing pain and speed up the healing process.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24 hours to verify that the infection is not spreading past the penned line and the fever is resolving.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness rapidly spreads past the ink line, if you develop extreme, unbearable pain, if purple blisters form on the skin, or if you become confused or dizzy upon standing, present immediately to the nearest Emergency Department via ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Cellulitis can occasionally fail oral antibiotics and require intravenous treatment in the hospital if it continues to spread.
Clinical Course: The fever and chills should resolve within 24-48 hours. The redness may temporarily look slightly worse when you stand up due to blood pooling, but the overall size should stop expanding within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the penned line on your leg closely. Seek immediate emergency care if the redness spreads rapidly beyond it or if you begin vomiting and cannot keep the antibiotics down.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Bacteria from an insect bite have invaded the deep layers of your skin. The high-dose antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but keeping the leg elevated is equally important to reduce the fluid build-up that is stretching your skin.
Actionable Steps: Keep the leg elevated above heart level. Take the antibiotics continuously until the course is finished, even if you feel better.

